  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mohammad.K vs The Food Inspector on 20 September, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Tuesday, The 20Th Day Of September 2016/29Th Bhadra, 1938 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1308 of 2009 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 251/2005 of Additionalsessions Court (Adhoc), Manjeri Stc 1382/2002 of J.M.F.C.-I, Perinthalmanna Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused No.1: --------------------------------------------------------------------- Mohammad.K.,S/O.Saidalavi. Kanakkancheri House,Aliparamba P.O. Anamangad (Via), Perinthalmanna Taluk,, Malappuram District. By Advs.Sri.T.K.Saidalikutty Smt.Anitha M.N. (Ekm) Sri.A.A.Ziyad Rahman Respondent/Respondent/Complainant And The State: ---------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. The Food Inspector, Perinthalmanna Municipality. 2. The State Of Kerala, Rep.By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam.",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 28.8.2001 at about 2.50 p.m., PW3, the Food Inspector, purchased three packets of banana chips, each packet contained 250 grams each, from the revision petitioner. The second accused was the licensee of the said shop. After complying with the legal formalities, one sample was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P17 report stating that the sample contained tartarzine, which is a synthetic colour and hence, the sample was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW3 were examined and Exts.P1 to P23 were marked for the prosecution. Ext.D1 and D2 were marked on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has argued that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P17 report of the Public Analyst cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6 It was held by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector [2016 (2) KLT 390] considered the above question on a reference by a learned Single Judge. The Division Bench in Rasheed (supra) held that if an analysis is required to initiate prosecution, there should be notified laboratory and validated method of analysis as mandated under Section 23(1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act for a successful prosecution Mohammad. K vs The Food Inspector on 20 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/70926779/ 2 as held by the Apex Court in Pepsico Holdings (supra).",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has argued that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P17 report of the Public Analyst cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "6 It was held by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector [2016 (2) KLT 390] considered the above question on a reference by a learned Single Judge. The Division Bench in Rasheed (supra) held that if an analysis is required to initiate prosecution, there should be notified laboratory and validated method of analysis as mandated under Section 23(1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act for a successful prosecution Mohammad. K vs The Food Inspector on 20 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/70926779/ 2 as held by the Apex Court in Pepsico Holdings (supra)."
  },
